# FAR-SIDE-BINARIES - every compiled thing a station is allowed to need
#
# This file is the policy, not a note about it. `.github/workflows/validate.yml`
# reads it, and a program under `cmd/` whose name appears anywhere under
# `station/` and is NOT listed here fails the build.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. The rule used to be "never a binary on the far side", and
# heliograph-seal was already one - argued for in the relay spec, and passing CI
# only because it is built from `cmd/` rather than existing as Go source under
# `station/`. One exception is an exception. Two, arriving the same way, is a
# policy nobody wrote down. So the policy is written down: AGENTS.md, "The
# boundary, and what it now costs to cross it".
#
# WHAT LISTING ONE COSTS. "Read it before you run it" stops working the moment
# a station needs something compiled, and it is replaced by "verify the binary
# matches the source you read" - which is only true if the build is
# reproducible and the checksum is published. So a binary may be added here
# only when all three hold:
#
#   1. the transport that needs it genuinely cannot be done in bash, and the
#      argument is written in a spec, not in a commit message
#   2. `packaging/reproduce.sh` builds it, so anybody can rebuild the exact
#      bytes from the tag and check them against the published SHA256SUMS
#   3. the station verifies it by hash before running it, and refuses on a
#      mismatch. RELAY_SEAL_SHA256 is the shape
#
# AND WHAT IT DOES NOT COST. No estate loses heliograph over this. The git,
# share, bundle and object-store transports are pure bash and always will be;
# beacon and flare are a complete product with no binary anywhere on the far
# side. What an estate that forbids compiled code loses is the relay, and the
# beam when it lands - two shapes, not the tool.
#
# One name per line. Blank lines and # comments are ignored.

# heliograph-seal - the relay transport, on a bash station.
#
# The relay is end-to-end encrypted so that the relay operator (us) cannot read
# a log, which needs X25519, ChaCha20-Poly1305, Ed25519 and HKDF on the far
# side. curl and coreutils do not do those, and a bash implementation of any of
# them would be bespoke cryptography, which AGENTS.md refuses outright.
#
# The PowerShell station needs no such thing: `station/powershell/lib/seal.psm1`
# ships the same construction as SOURCE and compiles it at startup with
# Add-Type, so an estate that permits no binary at all can still use the relay
# by running the PowerShell payload.
heliograph-seal
